INT. LUMEN NAIL BAR - AFTERNOON

The salon smells of acetone and jasmine, and under that of something else, something nobody in the room has named yet; the dryers hum in a steady chord, the television above the pedicure chairs plays a cooking show on mute, and at the third station a woman in a grey hoodie sits with both hands flat on the table as though she were waiting for them to be read.

NORA
(filing, not looking up)
Any plans for the weekend, hon?

THE WOMAN
(voice flat, eyes on the window)
Not really. Maybe visit my sister. She lives out past the reservoir.

NORA
(brightly)
Oh that's nice. Family is everything.

THE WOMAN
(beat)
Is it.

Nora glances up. Behind the counter, VIVIAN rings up a customer, tucking a stray lock behind her ear, and at the far chair THE TWINS, identical in matching pink smocks, murmur to each other in a rhythm too even to be conversation.

THE TWINS
(together, softly)
Do you smell it? Do you smell it?

VIVIAN
(not turning, counting change)
Girls, enough with the perfume jokes.

THE TWINS
(together)
Not perfume.

NORA
(forcing a laugh)
They get like this when it rains. Ignore them.

THE WOMAN
(finally looking at Nora)
It's not raining.

A silence. The cooking show on the television flickers, goes to static, comes back. In the corner, DEV the new tech, who has been sweeping the same square of floor for ten minutes, stops.

DEV
(low, to Vivian)
The wifi's dropped again. And the card reader. Everything just--

VIVIAN
(sharply, not turning)
Dev. Floor.

DEV
(obeying, quieter)
Sorry. Sorry.

NORA
(trying again, painting a clear coat)
So, what do you do, hon? For work?

THE WOMAN
(a thin smile)
I count things. How many people, how many exits. How long the lights stay on.

NORA
(her brush stilling)
That's a strange job.

THE WOMAN
(beat)
It's a strange week.

The Twins stop murmuring. Both rise from their chairs at the same instant, smocks rustling, and the room tilts toward them.

THE TWINS
(together, calm)
She is early.

VIVIAN
(finally turning, color draining)
Who is early?

THE TWINS
(together, pointing at the hoodie)
Her. She was not supposed to come until Thursday.

NORA
(whispering, to the woman)
Hon, what are they talking about?

THE WOMAN
(pushing back her hood to reveal pale, almost translucent skin)
My name is Ilsa. And I am so sorry. I wanted one last manicure before the end.

DEV
(dropping the broom)
The end of what?

ILSA
(rising, her hands beginning to glow faintly under the dryer lamps)
Of this. All of it. They said you'd want to know.

VIVIAN
(very quiet)
Nobody here asked.

ILSA
(gently)
Nobody ever does.

The television goes black. Every dryer in the salon stops at once, and in the sudden quiet a single drop of polish falls from Nora's brush and rings against the tabletop like a bell.
